 ${x}^{7}y$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{8}{1}){x}^{8-1}(2y)^{1}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{8}{1}){x}^{8-1}(2y)^{1} & =8{x}^{7}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =16{x}^{7}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{7}y$ is $16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-18627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cedad404b947dcd4b461bd2c511fd0b1c79556cfa346f0e8fc0f3d13aacc2120"} {"question" "q-18627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-18627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "112"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "30"] ["math" "32"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-18627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-113621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ in the expansion of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}$ is" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-113621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10},$ we have $a=2,$ $b={x}^{3},$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}=(\\frac{10}{0}){2}^{10}{({x}^{3})}^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){2}^{10-1}{({x}^{3})}^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){2}^{10-2}{({x}^{3})}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{10}{10}){2}^{10-10}{({x}^{3})}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{18}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6} & =210\\cdot {2}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =210\\cdot 16\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =3\\,360{x}^{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ is $3\\,360.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-113621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cedad404b947dcd4b461bd2c511fd0b1c79556cfa346f0e8fc0f3d13aacc2120"} {"question" "q-113621" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-113621" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\,680"] ["math" "210"] ["math" "3\\,360"] ["math" "420"] ["math" "5\\,040"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-113621" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\,360"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-18625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The coefficient of ${x}^{7}{y}^{3}$ in the expansion of ${(x+7y)}^{10}$ is" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-18625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x+7y)}^{10},$ we have $a=x,$ $b=7y,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+7y)}^{10}=(\\frac{10}{0}){x}^{10}(7y)^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){x}^{10-1}(7y)^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){x}^{10-2}(7y)^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{10}{10}){x}^{10-10}(7y)^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{7}{y}^{3}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{10}{3}){x}^{10-3}(7y)^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{10}{3}){x}^{10-3}(7y)^{3} & =120{x}^{7}\\cdot {7}^{3}{y}^{3} \\\\  & =120{x}^{7}\\cdot 343{y}^{3} \\\\  & =41\\,160{x}^{7}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{7}{y}^{3}$ is $41\\,160.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-18625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cedad404b947dcd4b461bd2c511fd0b1c79556cfa346f0e8fc0f3d13aacc2120"} {"question" "q-18625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-18625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "40\\,150"] ["math" "40\\,500"] ["math" "41\\,160"] ["math" "41\\,680"] ["math" "42\\,520"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "q-18625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "41\\,160"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/state.json" "file_sha256" "852634ef6e7f62cba2b131730816f1a94a75970c2e3507b71abae2600247adaf"} {"question" "e-5153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expand ${( 3x + y )}^3$ using the binomial theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/example-5153.json" "file_sha256" "30bb29c03fdf31e004b48d61ad2a3dc742b02f6e647c7f3905ca9d6869347f29"} {"question" "e-5153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(3x +y)}^3,$ we have  $a=3x,$ $b=y,$ and $n=3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can expand ${(3x + y)}^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+y)^{3} & =(\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(y)^{0}+(\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{3-1}(y)^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{3-2}(y)^{2}+(\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{3-3}(y)^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(1)+(\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{2}(y)^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{1}(y)^{2}+(\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{0}(y)^{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3x)^{3}+3\\cdot (3x)^{2}(y)+3\\cdot (3x)(y)^{2}+1\\cdot 1\\cdot ({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =27{x}^{3}+27{x}^{2}y+9x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/example-5153.json" "file_sha256" "30bb29c03fdf31e004b48d61ad2a3dc742b02f6e647c7f3905ca9d6869347f29"} {"question" "e-9775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${( x - 3y )}^ 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/example-9775.json" "file_sha256" "1d540d7056e1014708fa93854f7ec820f39e70bf4546efc3f9e8a966eb6bb712"} {"question" "e-9775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5,$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-3y ,$ and $n=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3y)^{5} & =(\\frac{5}{0})(x)^{5}(-3y)^{0}+(\\frac{5}{1})(x)^{5-1}(-3y)^{1}+(\\frac{5}{2})(x)^{5-2}(-3y)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(x)^{5}+5(x)^{4}(-3y)+10(x)^{3}(9{y}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & ={x}^{5}-15{x}^{4}y+90{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/example-9775.json" "file_sha256" "1d540d7056e1014708fa93854f7ec820f39e70bf4546efc3f9e8a966eb6bb712"} {"question" "e-5154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the coefficient of $x^{4}$ in the expansion of ${\\left( {3 + x} \\right)^{7}} .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/example-5154.json" "file_sha256" "7d15a2461f9e0b70c50f360305648b4aa96a39f5e4e8fe680a84cb3225066f4c"} {"question" "e-5154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${\\left( {3 +x} \\right)^{7}},$ we have $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+x)^{7}=(\\frac{7}{0}){3}^{7}{x}^{0}+(\\frac{7}{1}){3}^{7-1}{x}^{1}+(\\frac{7}{2}){3}^{7-2}{x}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{7}{7}){3}^{7-7}{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the $x^4$ term must be\n\n$$\n{7 \\choose 4}{3^{7 - 4}}{x^4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{7}{4}){3}^{7-4} & =35\\cdot {3}^{3} \\\\  & =35\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =945.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $x^{4}$ is $945.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988467/example-5154.json" "file_sha256" "7d15a2461f9e0b70c50f360305648b4aa96a39f5e4e8fe680a84cb3225066f4c"}] "usage" {"e-5154" [] "q-18627" [] "e-9775" [] "q-19353" [] "q-135895" [] "e-5153" []} "basis" 968 "retractions" [[17592186101051 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n$(3 + x)^{5} =$"] [17592186101209 :question/problem "What is $(x - 2y)^{6}$?"] [17592186101197 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe coefficient of $x^{7}y$ in the expansion of $(x + 2y)^{8}$ is"] [17592186100630 :question/problem "Expand ${(3x + y)}^3$ using the binomial theorem."] [17592186100630 :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n$, we have\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(3x +y)}^3$, we have $a=3x$, $b=y$, and $n=3$.\n\nTherefore, we can expand ${(3x + y)}^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3x + y)^{3} &= (\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(y)^{0} + (\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{3 - 1}(y)^{1} + (\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{3 - 2}(y)^{2} + (\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{3 - 3}(y)^{3} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(1) + (\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{2}(y)^{1} + (\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{1}(y)^{2} + (\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{0}(y)^{3} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot (3x)^{3} + 3 \\cdot (3x)^{2}(y) + 3 \\cdot (3x)(y)^{2} + 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (y^{3}) \\\\\n&= 27x^{3} + 27x^{2}y + 9xy^{2} + y^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186100709 :question/problem "Find the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5$."] [17592186100709 :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n$, we have\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5$, we have $a=x$, $b=-3y$, and $n=5$.\n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 3y)^{5} &= (\\frac{5}{0})(x)^{5}(-3y)^{0} + (\\frac{5}{1})(x)^{5 - 1}(-3y)^{1} + (\\frac{5}{2})(x)^{5 - 2}(-3y)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1(x)^{5} + 5(x)^{4}(-3y) + 10(x)^{3}(9y^{2}) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= x^{5} - 15x^{4}y + 90x^{3}y^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186100631 :question/problem "Find the coefficient of $x^{4}$ in the expansion of ${\\left({3 + x} \\right)^{7}}$."] [17592186100631 :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n$, we have\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nIn the case of\n\n$$\n{\\left({3 +x} \\right)^{7}}\n$$\n\nwe have $a=3$, $b=x$, and $n=7$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n(3 + x)^{7} = (\\frac{7}{0})3^{7}x^{0} + (\\frac{7}{1})3^{7 - 1}x^{1} + (\\frac{7}{2})3^{7 - 2}x^{2} + ⋯ + (\\frac{7}{7})3^{7 - 7}x^{7}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the $x^4$ term must be\n\n$$\n{7 \\choose 4}{3^{7 - 4}}{x^4}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{7}{4})3^{7 - 4} &= 35 \\cdot 3^{3} \\\\\n&= 35 \\cdot 27 \\\\\n&= 945\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $x^{4}$ is $945$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186098077 17592186098078 17592186098079 17592186098080 17592186098081 17592186098939 17592186098940 17592186098941 17592186098942 17592186098943 17592186099011 17592186099012 17592186099013 17592186099014 17592186099015] "immutable_fields" [[17592186098076 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098076 :answer-field/id] [17592186098076 :answer-field/key] [17592186098076 :answer-field/type] [17592186098938 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098938 :answer-field/id] [17592186098938 :answer-field/key] [17592186098938 :answer-field/type] [17592186099010 :answer-field/correct] [17592186099010 :answer-field/id] [17592186099010 :answer-field/key] [17592186099010 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
